# Erdős Problem #99 — second-layer penetration or internal-shell tangency

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Target:** C-II, canonical full-core Type-II branch  
**Depends on:** matched `g=0.0050` penetration matching/deep purity; relative-interior full-core KKT stress; triangle-free unit-contact angle separation; Section-124 fixed-scale loaded-hull flatness  
**Claim class:** proof-only source-level theorem awaiting independent audit

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The matched tooth branch now contains a penetration matching

\[
M=\{xw\}
\]

of size

\[
|M|\ge ZD-o(D),
\qquad
Z=0.3948477885367828447\ldots .
\]

Here `x` is a shallow unloaded tooth apex and `w` is a distinct deep unloaded endpoint.
Every `w` has no loaded contact neighbor and has internal contact degree between three
and five.  Thus, besides its matched edge `wx`, it has at least two further internal
contacts.

KKT equilibrium gives more than that degree count.  The vector from `w` back to `x`
has a fixed **outward** normal component.  Since `w` carries no diameter reaction, at
least one of its other internal contacts must point inward.  Selecting one such
balancing contact per `w` gives `|M|` directed internal incidences.

At the fixed threshold

\[
\lambda={13\over25}=0.52,
\]

those incidences force one of two weight-free source structures:

1. a new second layer of at least `|M|/4` distinct vertices at depth greater than
   `sqrt(3)/2+13/25`; or
2. at least `|M|/4` distinct internal contacts whose direction lies within
   `arcsin(13/25)` of the local loaded-hull tangent.

Numerically, at the current matched endpoint,

\[
\boxed{
{|M|\over4D}
\ge0.0987119471341957111\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{SL2.1}

This is the first forced continuation **past** the attachment-free deep matching
layer.  It does not use a lower bound on any KKT coefficient.

---

## 1. Every deep endpoint has an inward balancing contact

Fix one matching edge `xw` and orient the local normal `n_w` inward from the loaded
base side associated with the tooth of `x`.  The shallow-tooth theorem gives

\[
(w-x)\cdot n_w>Y_{1/15}>0.709,
\]

so, viewed from `w`,

\[
(x-w)\cdot n_w<-0.709.
\tag{SL2.2}

The deep-purity theorem says every contact of `w` is internal to its unloaded
positive-contact component.  In particular `w` has no diameter reaction.  In the
relative-interior full-core KKT stress, every incident positive contact has coefficient
strictly positive and equilibrium at `w` is

\[
\sum_{y:\,wy\in E_C}\alpha_{wy}(y-w)=0.
\tag{SL2.3}

Taking scalar product with `n_w`, the strictly negative matched contribution in
(SL2.2) cannot be cancelled if every other contact has nonpositive inward component.
Therefore there exists at least one contact

\[
wy(w)\ne wx
\]

with

\[
\boxed{(y(w)-w)\cdot n_w>0.}
\tag{SL2.4}

Choose one such balancing contact for every `w`.  Since the matching has distinct deep
endpoints, this produces exactly `|M|` directed selected incidences.

---

## 2. Strong inward selections produce genuinely new deep vertices

Call the selected incidence at `w` **strong** if

\[
(y-w)\cdot n_w\ge\lambda={13\over25}.
\tag{SL2.5}

The matched deep endpoint has depth

\[
\operatorname{depth}(w)>\sqrt3/2
\]

relative to its tooth-base support line.  Therefore a strong target satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{depth}(y)
> {\sqrt3\over2}+{13\over25}
=1.3860254037\ldots .
}
\tag{SL2.6}

By contrast, every endpoint in the first two matching layers has, relative to its own
nearby loaded support line,

\[
\operatorname{depth}(x)\le{\sqrt{59}\over30}=0.25603819\ldots,
\]

or

\[
\operatorname{depth}(w)
<1+{\sqrt{59}\over30}+o(1)
=1.25603819\ldots+o(1).
\]

A strong target is at contact distance one from `w`, so all relevant loaded base sides
lie in one fixed Euclidean neighborhood.  Section-124 fixed-scale flatness makes their
normals and support levels differ by `o(1)`.  Since the fixed gap

\[
{\sqrt3\over2}+{13\over25}
-\left(1+{\sqrt{59}\over30}\right)
>0.1299
\tag{SL2.7}
\]

is positive, for all sufficiently large `D` a strong target belongs to neither first
matching endpoint class `X` nor `W`.  It is a genuinely new deeper unloaded vertex.

---

## 3. One new deep vertex receives at most two strong selections

Fix a source vertex `y` receiving strong selected contacts from distinct deep endpoints
`w_1,...,w_r`.  Since `|y-w_i|=1` and every `w_i` is within bounded distance of its
loaded base, all associated base sides lie in one fixed-scale boundary window.  Their
inward normals agree with one common normal `n` up to `o(1)`.

From (SL2.5), the contact directions viewed from `y` satisfy

\[
(w_i-y)\cdot n\le-{13\over25}+o(1).
\tag{SL2.8}

Thus all rays `yw_i` lie in an outward cone of asymptotic half-angle

\[
\arccos(13/25).
\]

Its total angular width is

\[
2\arccos(13/25)<{2\pi\over3}.
\tag{SL2.9}

Any two distinct unit-contact neighbors of `y` are mutually unit-separated, so their
angular separation at `y` is at least `pi/3`.  Three such rays would require total span
at least `2pi/3`, contradicting (SL2.9) for large `D`.  Hence

\[
\boxed{
\text{one target vertex receives at most two strong selected incidences.}
}
\tag{SL2.10}

If `S` is the number of strong selected incidences, they therefore reach at least

\[
\boxed{S/2}
\tag{SL2.11}

**distinct new second-layer vertices** satisfying (SL2.6).

---

## 4. Weak inward selections are near-tangent internal-shell contacts

A selected incidence is **weak** when

\[
0<(y-w)\cdot n_w<{13\over25}.
\tag{SL2.12}

Because `|y-w|=1`, its angle from one of the two local tangent directions is less than

\[
\boxed{
\theta_{sh}:=\arcsin(13/25)
=0.54685095\ldots\text{ rad}
=31.33\ldots^\circ.
}
\tag{SL2.13}

Thus weak selections are genuine near-tangential internal contacts in the fixed-width
unloaded shell.

A physical contact edge has at most two endpoints in `W`, so it can support at most two
selected weak incidences.  If `|M|-S` selections are weak, they therefore contain at
least

\[
\boxed{(|M|-S)/2}
\tag{SL2.14}

**distinct** weak inward near-tangent internal edges.

No directional coherence between far-separated hull windows is assumed; tangency is
local to the associated tooth support direction.

---

## 5. The second-layer dichotomy

Equations (SL2.11) and (SL2.14) give

\[
\max\left\{
\#\text{new second-layer vertices},
\#\text{weak near-tangent internal edges}
\right\}
\ge {1\over2}\max\{S,|M|-S\}
\ge {|M|\over4}.
\]

Therefore every matched tooth survivor satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{either}&
\ge |M|/4\text{ distinct new unloaded vertices at depth }>
\sqrt3/2+13/25,\\[1mm]
\text{or}&
\ge |M|/4\text{ distinct internal unit contacts within }
\arcsin(13/25)\text{ of the local tangent.}
\end{array}
}
\tag{SL2.15}

At the matched `g=0.0050` endpoint, `|M|/D>=Z-o(1)` with

\[
Z=0.3948477885367828447\ldots,
\]

so both alternatives have the uniform coefficient in (SL2.1).

---

## 6. Frontier effect

The tooth branch can no longer terminate at one penetration edge per shallow tooth.
Every one of the `0.3948D` attachment-free deep matched endpoints must continue its
contact network inward or tangentially:

```text
first penetration matching M: >=0.3948477 D

then either
    >=0.0987119 D genuinely new vertices beyond depth 1.386,
or
    >=0.0987119 D distinct near-tangent internal shell contacts.
```

The second alternative is a new inner-shell analogue of the outer long-side branch;
the first gives a third vertex layer at a fixed extra depth.  Iterating the same idea
would require controlling how the local support direction evolves after one leaves
the fixed-width Section-124 window, so no iteration is asserted here.

This theorem remains weight-free except for using positivity in the local KKT
balance.  It does not lower-bound selected coefficients and does not yet produce a
strict diameter descent.  C-II remains open.

## 7. Scope audit

- KKT is used only at deep unloaded endpoints, which carry no diameter reaction.
- All incident contacts at a deep endpoint are internal by the deep-purity theorem.
- The selected balancing contact may depend on the KKT stress; only its existence and
  geometry are used afterward.
- The target-multiplicity bound uses source unit separation and fixed-scale hull
  flatness, not contact-degree bookkeeping alone.
- The first/second-layer depth comparison is made only for vertices at bounded mutual
  distance, so the Section-124 local support transfer is applicable.
- The near-tangent branch counts distinct physical internal contact edges.
- Contact-isolated source points remain in the source.
- C-II and Level I remain open.
